The M340 module now pulls SNMP data using Schneider Electric's Transparent Ready private MIB (``TFPRIVATE-MIB``, compiled without the MIB texts), with proper data types, enumeration labels and decoded table indices, walking the equipment profile (including the LED and trap tables), Port 502 messaging, I/O scanning, Global Data, web, address server, NTP and email groups that the agent advertises, plus ``ifTable`` and ``ipAddrTable``. The results fill in the device's interfaces, services, module status, uptime and related peer IPs (the full data is in ``extra.snmp``), values the MIB can't decode are kept as raw OIDs instead of aborting the pull, and the pull stops walking once the agent stops responding. The new ``m340.pull_methods`` device option (``ftp``, ``snmp`` and ``modbus_tcp``) selects the network services used to pull additional information from M340 devices; removing ``snmp`` disables the SNMP identification method and the SNMP pull for them (e.g. for devices affected by CVE-2022-0222). The new ``snmp.max_varbinds`` device option limits the number of SNMP requests per walked MIB subtree (only the M340 module uses it). ``peat.protocols.snmp.SNMP`` gained ``load_mibs()``, ``walk()``, ``get_table()``, ``last_error`` and ``timed_out``, and responses now include the typed ``value``, ``table`` and ``index_parts`` of each object.
